    "stop_reason": "success",
    "result_text": "The full suite is green (`BUILD SUCCESS`; the project's only test is `AcceptanceTest`, which now passes).\n\n## Summary\n\nI implemented soft-match \"possible duplicate\" detection for `POST /api/owners`. A new owner that isn't a hard duplicate but shares an existing owner's last name and postcode with a *different* telephone is still created and flagged.\n\n**Changes:**\n\n1. **`Owner` entity** (`model/Owner.java`) \u2014 added persisted fields `possibleDuplicate` (`Boolean`, column `possible_duplicate`) and `possibleDuplicateOf` (`Integer`, column `possible_duplicate_of`) with getters/setters, following the existing `namesakeCount` pattern.\n\n2. **DB schemas** \u2014 added the two columns to the `owners` table in all four `schema.sql` files (hsqldb \u2014 used by tests \u2014 plus h2, mysql, postgres).\n\n3. **OpenAPI** (`openapi.yml`) \u2014 added read-only `possibleDuplicate` / `possibleDuplicateOf` properties to the `Owner` schema, so the generated `OwnerDto` carries them and MapStruct maps them by name automatically.\n\n4. **New function** `AssignPossibleDuplicate.java` \u2014 runs after `BuildOwner` (owner already has E.164 telephone + postcode). It scans existing owners for one with the same normalized last name and postcode but a different telephone; on a match sets `possibleDuplicate = true` and `possibleDuplicateOf = <matching id>`, otherwise `false`. Hard duplicates are already rejected upstream by `EnsureUniqueIdentity`, so anything reaching this step is a genuine soft match.\n\n5. **Pipeline wiring** (`owners.POST.yml`) \u2014 inserted the `assignPossibleDuplicate` step (under `govern: [ transaction ]`) between `assignBulkSignupWarning` and `save`.\n\nThe values persist on the entity, so `GET /api/owners/{id}` returns them via the existing mapper \u2014 exactly what the acceptance test asserts.",
